{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:10:27.835Z","data":{"turn":48,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Excellent! I now have comprehensive information about all the SSRF sinks. Let me compile the findings and update the todo list:\\n\\n1. **POST /api/webhooks/test** - VULNERABLE (no URL validation, response returned, authenticated) - HIGH confidence\\n2. **GET /api/integrations/import** - VULNERABLE (no URL validation, response returned, authenticated) - HIGH confidence\\n3. **POST /api/messages/conversations/:id/link-preview** - VULNERABLE (partial blocklist - only blocks localhost and 127.0.0.1, many bypass vectors exist, response returned) - HIGH confidence\\n\\n\\n4. **GET /api/contracts/:id/invoice** - VULNERABLE (Puppeteer HTML injection via display_name/milestone.title, --no-sandbox, networkidle0) - HIGH confidence, though it requires owning a contract with a specific display_name\\n5. **POST /api/disputes/:id/evidence** - evidence_url is stored in the database but never fetched server-side, so not an SSRF vulnerability\\n\\nNow I'm pinpointing the exact code locations for each vulnerability, starting with the webhook test endpoint in webhook.service.js where the HTTP request happens.\\n\\nFor the integrations import flow, the vulnerable request is also in webhook.service.js but at a different location. The link-preview endpoint in messaging.service.js only blocks localhost and 127.0.0.1, leaving other internal addresses exposed. For the PDF generation, the Puppeteer sink accepts user-controlled display_name and milestone.title without sanitization before rendering with networkidle0. Let me verify the exact line numbers from the provided code. Now I'm confirming that all four endpoints are exploitable from the internet since they only require authenticated user access, which can be obtained by registering and logging in externally. I'm ready to generate the deliverable files documenting these findings.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:10:27.835Z"}}
